# PRIME ATLAS

Mathematical Foundations — Scientific Formulation v1.0

Subject: Prime wheel geometry, modular phase oscillators, resonance detection, and recursive arithmetic structure.

Objective: Establish a unified mathematical system describing the distribution of integers that survive successive prime-factor elimination.

## I. Fundamental definitions

Let \\(p_k\\) denote the \\(k\\)-th prime, with \\(p_1=2\\).

Define the primorial wheel modulus:

\\[ \boxed{W_k=\prod\_{j=1}^{k}p_j} \\]

Define its surviving residue set:

\\[ \boxed{ \mathcal R_k= \\{r\in\\{1,\ldots,W_k\\}:\gcd(r,W_k)=1\\} } \\]

The number of survivors is

\\[ \boxed{ S_k=|\mathcal R_k|=\varphi(W_k) =\prod\_{j=1}^{k}(p_j-1). } \\]

The surviving integers are precisely those not divisible by any of the first \\(k\\) primes.

## II. Wheel geometry and evolution

Represent the ordered survivors as

\\[ 1=r_1\<r_2<\cdots\<r\_{S_k}\<W_k. \\]

Define the cyclic gap sequence:

\\[ \boxed{ g_i= \begin{cases} r\_{i+1}-r_i,&i\<S_k,\\\ W_k+r_1-r\_{S_k},&i=S_k. \end{cases} } \\]

Two fundamental identities follow:

\\[ \boxed{ \sum\_{i=1}^{S_k}g_i=W_k } \\]

\\[ \boxed{ W\_{k+1}=p\_{k+1}W_k,\qquad S\_{k+1}=(p\_{k+1}-1)S_k. } \\]

The recursive wheel transformation consists of replicating the old residue pattern \\(p\_{k+1}\\) times and removing multiples of \\(p\_{k+1}\\).

